#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""kilix-stt — dictation settings, and the answer to "why can't it hear me?".

Two jobs share one screen.  The first is ordinary: choose the microphone, the
recogniser, the model, and the rules a dictation turn runs under, and write
them to the settings document the whole Kilix stack shares.  The second is the
reason this tool exists — a live level meter fed by the same ``MicCapture`` and
``Vad`` a real turn uses.  A microphone that is muted, missing, plugged into
the wrong socket, turned up so far that it clips, or quietly recording the
speakers instead of the room is diagnosed here, in the units the detector
itself works in, rather than guessed at from a dictation turn that produced no
text.

The meter is a diagnostic, not a recording.  Nothing is recognised, nothing is
written to disk, and the recorder stops on the same ceiling
(``KILIX_VOICE_STT_MAX_SECONDS``) that bounds a real turn.  Nothing opens the
microphone until Space is pressed: starting this program touches no audio
device, which is what lets it run on a machine that has none.

Models are listed before they are fetched. An explicit install action hands the
terminal to Kilix's pinned installer (or to the pinned Bonsai store for its
shared VibeVoice weights); merely opening this screen never starts a transfer.

It is also a plain CLI, which is what scripts and Kilix's own chrome use:

    kilix-stt --print
    kilix-stt --models
    kilix-stt --install lgraph-en-us --default lgraph-en-us
    kilix-stt --set device=alsa_input.pci-0000_00_1f.3.analog-stereo
    kilix-stt --set submit=confirm --set max_seconds=60
"""

